course: dictionary
lesson: 7962
title: How the body notices the world
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A receptor is a part of your body that notices something and passes the news on. Your eyes, nose, tongue and skin all have receptors. Each one picks up one kind of signal, such as light, smell, taste or touch.
explain: «A dog's nose has hundreds of millions of smell receptors, while a human nose has about 5 or 6 million.» So a dog's nose has far more smell-catching parts than ours. More receptors means more chances to notice a smell.
explain: Receptors send a message to the brain, and the brain decides what the message means. «Fish have pain receptors (nerve ends that sense harm)» shows that some receptors are for sensing harm, so the body can react to an injury.
explain: A receptor can also mean a tiny place on a cell where a special chemical fits, like a key in a lock. Medicines often work this way. You will meet this meaning in more advanced science.
fact: A receptor is a part of the body that picks up a signal and sends a message to the brain.
fact: Taste receptors are on the tongue, light receptors are in the eye, and pain receptors are in the skin.
fact: Smell receptors help your nose pick up smells.
fact: A receptor can be a tiny place on a cell where a chemical, like a hormone or a medicine, fits like a key in a lock.
fact: Fish have pain receptors, and many experts think fish can feel pain.
exercise: «A dog's nose has hundreds of millions of smell receptors.» What do smell receptors do? | *Pick up smells and send a message to the brain | Make the nose run when it is cold | Make the air smell nicer
exercise: Which part picks up light and sends a message to the brain? | *Light receptors in the eye | Bones in the leg | Toenails | Hair on the head
exercise: «Fish have pain receptors (nerve ends that sense harm).» What does this tell us? | Fish never get hurt | Fish only live in the sea | *Fish have nerve ends that sense harm | Fish swim very fast
test: Where are taste receptors found? | In the ears | In the toes | *On the tongue | Inside the heart
test: In the second meaning, what does a receptor fit like? | A car on a road | A ball in a net | *A key in a lock | A bird in a nest
exam: Why might a dog's nose find a smell better than ours? | *It has hundreds of millions of smell receptors | It cannot smell anything | It has no nose at all | It has fewer smell receptors
